\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{The three-layer cube in detail}

  \p{The [ordinary cube action](frap-000P) makes an outer face turn
  explicit, and the [twist and flip calculation](frap-000V) gives its
  orientation constraints. This raises the concrete question of whether
  those local constraints describe every legal state. The [corner and edge
  stabilizers](frap-000Y) connect these examples with transfer, while a
  complete description of the #{3\times3\times3} cube still needs corner
  and edge coordinates for every face move, with a fixed frame of reference.}

  \p{Three questions must be distinguished. Can a proposed arrangement be
  described in the chosen coordinates? Does it satisfy the constraints
  preserved by legal moves? Can it actually be reached from the solved
  state? A characterization of reachability must justify the last step as
  well as the necessary constraints. This is where permutation parity,
  orientation constraints, and constructive move sequences meet
  \citet{Sections 3.1--3.2}{salkinder2021nxnxn}. The examples above establish
  necessary constraints, not this constructive sufficiency.}

  \p{A description of the reachable states still leaves a practical
  problem: given one of them, how do we choose moves that solve it? The
  transition from characterizing states to constructing a solution leads
  naturally to [methods and search](frap-0005).}
